{
  "dom_id": "step-q164333",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The table below gives the values of ${\u03a6}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p=\u03a6(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z>z)=0.01.$\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${\u03a6}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "First, we express the given information in terms of $\u03a6,$ using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>z) & =0.01 \\\\ 1-P(Z\\le z) & =0.01 \\\\ 1-\u03a6(z) & =0.01 \\\\ \u03a6(z) & =1-0.01 \\\\ \u03a6(z) & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz={\u03a6}^{-1}(0.99).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.99,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np=0.99\\,\u21d2\\,{\u03a6}^{-1}(p)=2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz={\u03a6}^{-1}(0.99)=2.3263.\n$$",
